**Why specification search inflates false positives.** A researcher who can choose among outcomes, control sets, subsamples and exclusion rules runs many tests and reports one. If $k$ candidate specifications were independent, the chance that at least one gives $p<0.05$ when there is no effect is $1-0.95^k$: 23% for $k=5$, 40% for $k=10$, 64% for $k=20$. Real specifications are correlated, so each adds less, but the menu is usually large. Simmons, Nelson and Simonsohn (2011) showed by simulation that combining four common choices (two outcome measures, optional extra data collection, an optional covariate, dropping a condition) raised the false-positive rate to 61%.

The search need not be deliberate. Gelman and Loken's 'garden of forking paths' is the case where the analyst makes one reasonable choice after seeing the data, and would have made a different reasonable choice had the data looked different.

Remedies, roughly in order of strength:

- a pre-analysis plan that fixes the primary outcome and specification;
- reporting every defensible specification, as a specification curve (Simonsohn, Simmons and Nelson 2020) or multiverse analysis (Steegen et al. 2016);
- holding out part of the sample for confirmation;
- multiple-testing corrections when several outcomes are of real interest (see the [MHT section](#mht)).
